        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Astronauts exposed to microgravity conditions have been noted to develop a spectrum of neurologic and ophthalmic disorders, including symptoms of idiopathic intracranial hypertension and papilledema. Translaminar pressure gradients between the intraocular and intracranial spaces, with the latter demonstrating elevated pressure, are postulated to be responsible for some of the ocular symptoms associated with prolonged space travel. Methods for early detection of elevated intracranial pressure (ICP) while the astronaut is still traveling would enable decision-making regarding treatment of the astronaut in space versus return to earth before disease progression.
<p>
When ICP is increased, it can impact cranial nerves which can in turn reduce or alter the functional capabilities of these nerves, including the nerves required for eye movements. Thus by detecting functional changes in eye movement patterns, one could potentially identify elevated ICP. Eye tracking while watching a short film clip may be used to quantitate the physiologic impact of elevated ICP without requiring a trained examiner, pupil dilatation, imaging studies, or an invasive procedure. This represents a new application of eye tracking technology and a new mechanism for assessment of elevated ICP that is non-invasive, automatable, and could potentially be performed remotely. The research study had two specific aims. (1) To determine eye tracking measures associated with measured ICP values in patients with brain injury and other lesions (e.g., hydrocephalus) causing elevated pressure admitted to the ICU for invasive neuromonitoring. (2) To demonstrate that subjects with mass effect (swelling) causing narrowing of the space adjacent to the third cranial nerve (perimesencephalic cistern or PMC) as assessed by volumetric analysis of computed tomography (CT) scan imaging have decreased vertical eye movement on tracking. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
This study was based in the Bellevue Hospital neurosurgical ICU in Manhattan, New York. In total, 24 patients (12 females, 12 males) were admitted to the neurosurgical intensive care unit for ICP monitoring and were enrolled in the study (mean age=47). Nineteen patients presented with hemorrhage, three with traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), 1 with intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH), and five with obstructive hydrocephalus. These 24 patients were eye-tracked in 78 separate sessions: 59 times with either ICP monitors or external ventriculostomy drains in place and actively monitoring ICP, and 19 times after discontinuation of monitoring or replacement of catheters by a ventriculoperitoneal shunt. Of the 24 patients, 18 were tracked serially at different ICPs. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This study demonstrated two major findings. The first is that eye tracking appears to be a viable approach for detecting the physiologic impact of elevated ICP. The second finding is that some patients demonstrate physiologic evidence of elevated ICP at values which are lower than those previously thought to represent thresholds for elevation. For example while 15 mm Hg is widely held to represent the value requiring treatment, the investigators show that some patients are symptomatic at levels as low as 7 or 8. This finding is consistent with healthy subjects’ human data which suggests that normal healthy subject ICP values are generally less than 5 mm Hg. This data further suggests that cellular microenvironment or brain tissue oxygenation may also impact physiologic cranial nerve function. 
<p>
        </div>

